diagnosis_text:
The network is fully healthy. Every layer of the path from host_1_1_1_1 to
web_server_2 (web2.local) and web_server_3 (web3.local) was verified and is
operating correctly.

DNS: The authoritative zone on dns_server (10.200.0.2) correctly maps
web2.local → 10.200.0.5 and web3.local → 10.200.0.6. nslookup from
host_1_1_1_1 resolves both names without error.

Data plane: ICMP ping from host_1_1_1_1 to both 10.200.0.5 and 10.200.0.6
completes with 0% packet loss. All OSPF routers (router_core_1/2/3,
switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, switch_server_access) carry complete
routing tables including the 10.200.0.0/24 server subnet.

Application layer: curl http://web2.local/ and http://web3.local/ from
host_1_1_1_1 both return HTTP 200 OK with the correct HTML body. The Apache
access logs on web_server_2 and web_server_3 confirm the server-side receipt
of those requests from 10.1.1.2 (host_1_1_1_1) with status 200.

No firewall or access-control rules exist on any device in the path (iptables
and ebtables are all policy ACCEPT with no rules). The br0 bridge on
switch_server_access correctly includes eth0–eth4 (covering all four web
servers and the DNS server).

Root cause: There is no anomaly. The user's report that web2.local and
web3.local are unreachable does not reflect the current state of the network.

Corrective action: No network change is required. The engineer should follow
up with the user to determine whether the issue was transient, whether it
involves a client-side browser setting (e.g., a cached redirect or a
browser-enforced HTTPS upgrade), or whether the complaint has been resolved.
